0030: the survey says how far the boot got

A survey boot that died early left no record at all: /Volumes/OPENCORE never appeared and
E:\NAVI48.TXT kept the bytes of the previous boot, so "the survey never ran" and "the survey
died at BAR5" looked identical on the stick. The writer thread now carries a progress record
beside the final one: survey_report.h defines kSurveyStageStart/Mmio/Discovery/Report as
cumulative bits and survey_stage_text() formats them as a 135-byte "navi48 survey progress:
start=1 mmio=0 discovery=0 report=0" line that keeps the "navi" magic the raw channel already
requires, and Navi48Bringup::start() marks each stage before the work it vouches for, through
survey_stage_mark() in accel_takeover.cpp - it starts the report thread on the first mark and
only ORs bits after it. The thread rewrites NAVI48.TXT whenever new bits arrive, so a boot that
stops at MMIO leaves "mmio=1 discovery=0" behind; the full record, marked kSurveyStageReport,
replaces the progress line exactly as patch 0029 wrote it. The mounted volume's grace drops
from 30 s to 1 s: this machine's survey boots never mounted the stick before the boot ended,
so 30 s of grace was 30 s of no report. Host suite: the E2 section pins the record's length
across bit values, the "navi" magic at every stage, that nothing is written past the cap, and
that each mark precedes the work it vouches for in start().

The tests come with it, and they are the only test-side change: the first full run showed the
takeover suite counting IOSleep(1000) over the whole of accel_takeover.cpp, a number 0027 and
0030 both moved, so its pins are now scoped to the function that owns them (the tick, the wait
constant, the stand-down on the positive signal, the census reads) and every thread that file
starts must still be released; survey_report_test.cpp pins the boot-arg gate (survey_stage_mark()
refuses without navi48-survey-report and consults no arming argument) and that the progress
record stays under the raw writer 1536-byte cap; and tests/accel_report_file_plant.sh plants
the volume misspelling against the path the header really holds, so its control step passes.

Review fixes, same patch number because 0030 was never booted. The review of the first 0030
build found four things that would have cost a boot each, and one of them was a correctness bug
in the record itself:

  * the writer's storage client was `this`, the bring-up service. Its whole reason to exist is
    the boot where start() returns false, and on that path IOKit stops and detaches the service -
    so the one boot that needed a report had a writer holding a service it could no longer use.
    All four hand-offs (the three stage marks and the final record) now pass the provider, which
    super::start() retains and the PCI registry keeps: kSurveyStageStart marks before pciDevice
    is even assigned, so it uses the argument, and the two later marks use pciDevice.
  * the survey handed the writer sizeof(text), the buffer, not n, the bytes the formatter wrote.
    The remaining ~1 KB of a kernel stack array went onto the stick as the tail of a tidy-looking
    survey record. It now passes n and the writer pads with spaces as before.
  * the 1 s grace turned the raw block-device write into a certainty on every survey boot - a
    data-cluster write into a FAT32 volume, started one second into a boot that had not finished
    mapping BAR5. That is now opt-in behind navi48-raw-report=1, read once at thread entry, so a
    boot can be run without it and the two outcomes differ only in that channel. A boot that
    resets with the arg off is therefore not the raw writer's fault; a boot that resets with it on
    is, and the panic log says so. The verdict reporter's own use of the raw channel is unchanged.
  * the survey re-checks gWriterStarted immediately before the complete record goes out, not only
    when it was handed over, because the verdict reporter can start after the survey's last
    progress line; and a failed kernel_thread_start() gives back both its retain and its claim, so
    the pending stage bits are picked up by the next mark instead of waiting for a thread that
    never ran.

survey_report_test.cpp grows 24 checks for exactly these: the pins that used to demand
`this, text, sizeof(text)` now refuse it, each mark is pinned to a client that outlives start()
and to its place in the function, raw_report_wanted() is pinned to one boot argument, and the
stage bits are pinned to be set before the thread is asked for. The two cross-builds that this
file only reaches through strings are what caught the unqualified kReportFilePath in the new log
lines - the host suites stayed green over it.
